Multiplication table. So we have 10 rows and in each row we have 10 columns. So for example, in the first row

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:21.860
we are working with the number 1 and you are printing 1 multiplied by 1, 2 multiplied by 1, 3 multiplied by 1, up until 10 multiplied by 1.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:26.740
When you finish from the first row, we are getting to a new line and now we are working with the number 2.

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:33.660
We are displaying 2 multiplied by 1 2 multiplied by 2 up until 2 multiplied by 10 and this will continue up until 10

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:39.140
We are going to display 10 multiplied by 1 10 multiplied by 2 up until 10 multiplied by 10

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:43.920
So over here we have an outer loop which is responsible for printing 10 lines, right?

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:50.980
And the inner loop is responsible for printing these values over here. We are printing 10 values in each row, okay?

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:56.140
So pause the video take your time and try to solve this exercise. Okay, so now let's see the solution

00:07:56.140 --> 00:08:01.580
The outer loop starts from i is equal to 1 as long as i is less than or equal to 10 and each time

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:06.620
we will increment i by 1. And this is because we have 10 rows and the first number is 1 and the

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:12.220
last number is 10, right? Now each time we want to multiply the number by all the numbers starting

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:17.660
from 1 up until 10. So over here we have another inner loop starting from 1 up until 10 and each

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:23.420
time we are incrementing j. And inside this loop we are going to print i multiplied by j followed

00:08:23.420 --> 00:08:28.620
followed by a space. So in the first iteration, i is equal to 1, right? So we'll start executing

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:34.520
the inner loop. So we are going to print 1 multiplied by 1, then 1 multiplied by 2, up

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:39.220
until 1 multiplied by 10. And after we finish executing the inner loop, we are going to

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:44.060
get a new line and increment i. So now i is equal to 2. So we'll do the same thing with

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:49.660
i equal 2. We are going to print 2 multiplied by 1, 2 multiplied by 2, up until 2 multiplied

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:54.760
by 10. And then we'll go to a new line. And this will continue up until i equal 10. We

00:08:54.760 --> 00:09:00.180
are going to print 10 multiplied by 1, 10 multiplied by 2, up until 10 multiplied by 10.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:04.540
And after that we'll increment i and now i is equal to 11. So 11 less than or equal

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:09.420
to 10, this is false. So we are done executing the outer loop. So for simplicity, our for

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:14.100
loop is responsible for the rows and our inner loop is responsible for the values or

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:19.380
for the columns in each row. Alright? Now have a look at this note over here. The name

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:23.200
of the variable in the outer loop must be different than the name of the variable in

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:28.200
the inner loop. So for example, suppose that you have a for loop and inside that you have

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:33.240
an integer i which is equal to 1. And inside this loop you have another loop and also we

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:37.320
